whole rook on offer."},{"title":"If Black takes the rook","text":"28…bxc5 is met by 29.Qxb7, taking the bishop on b7 and threatening Qxa8+, winning a rook. Then 30.Nc4 threatens Nxd6, winning a rook, and 32.d6 pushes the passed pawn to d6. White ends up a pawn ahead: White holds the balance (+0.01)."},{"title":"Black's best try","text":"Black's best is to leave the rook alone and play 28…Rad8. White answers 29.Rc7, and the rook on c5 gets away, threatening Rxe7, winning a bishop for a pawn. Then 30.Nxd5 takes the bishop on d5, and 31.Rxa7 takes the a7 pawn. White ends up a pawn ahead: White holds the balance (0.00)."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 28.Rcd1, is clearly weaker (−1.83 against 0.00). Black answers 28…Rad8, taking the half-open d-file, aiming at the d5 pawn behind the rook on d6. That would leave White clearly worse."}]}],"opening":{"name":"Catalan Opening: Open Defense, Modern Sharp Variation","eco":"E04","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 11…Nd5: 22 moves in all, 11 by White and 11 by Black.","White left the book with 12.Rc1. In 23 games from this position, strong players played 12.O-O (96%).","The main line goes on 12.O-O O-O.","From here White scored 48% in those games, with 5% drawn."]}}